==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|pollucion}}, from {{der|en|xno|pollutiun}}, {{der|en|frm|pollution}}, {{m|frm|pollucion}}, and their source, post-classical {{der|en|la|pollūtiō||defilement, desecration; nocturnal emission}} (4th century), from the participial stem of {{m|la|polluō||to soil, defile, contaminate}}, from {{m|la|por-||before}} + {{m|la||-luō|t=to smear}}, related to {{m|la|lutum||mud}} and {{m|la|luēs||filth}}. Compare {{cog|grc|λῦμα|t=filth, dirt, disgrace}} and {{m|grc|λῦμαξ|t=rubbish, refuse}}, {{cog|sga|loth|t=mud, dirt}}, {{cog|lt|lutynas|t=pool, puddle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/pəˈl(j)uːʃən/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/pəˈluʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-pollution.ogg|Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}
# Physical [[contamination]], now especially the [[contamination]] of the [[environment]] by [[harmful]] [[substance]]s, or by disruptive levels of noise, light etc. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Pollution''' levels are almost always higher in cities rather than the countryside, what with the cars, industry and so on.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=[[w:Edwin Black|Edwin Black]]|title=Internal Combustion
|chapter=1|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL4103950W
|passage=If successful, Edison and Ford—in 1914—would move society away from the{{...}}hazards of gasoline cars: air and water '''pollution''', noise and noxiousness, constant coughing and the undeniable rise in cancers caused by smoke exhaust particulates.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 July 2018|author=Matthew Taylor|journal=The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2018/jul/13/uk-schools-move-to-ban-the-school-run-to-protect-pupils-from-air-pollution
|passage=Schools across the country are moving to ban the school run amid growing concern about the devastating impact of air '''pollution''' on young people’s health.}}
#* '''2019''', George Monbiot, ''[https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2019/mar/07/cars-killing-us-driving-environment-phase-out Cars are killing us. Within 10 years, we must phase them out]'' in the Guardian.
#*: '''Pollution''' now kills three times as many people worldwide as Aids, tuberculosis and malaria combined.
# Something that [[pollute]]s; a [[pollutant]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} The [[desecration]] of something holy or sacred; [[defilement]], [[profanation]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|text=Men who attend the Altar, and should most / Endevor Peace: thir strife '''pollution''' brings / Upon the Temple it self […].|XII}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Innocents Abroad|text=[T]he most gallant knights that ever wielded sword wasted their lives away in a struggle to seize it and hold it sacred from infidel '''pollution'''.}}
# {{lb|en|now|archaic}} The [[ejaculation]] of [[semen]] outside of [[sexual intercourse]], ''especially'' a [[nocturnal emission]]. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#* '''1839''', Robley Dunglison, ''Medical Lexicon'', Blanchard, page 492:
#*:When occasioned by a voluntary act it is called, simply, '''''Pollution''''' or ''Masturbation'' (q.v.); when excited, during sleep, by lascivious dreams, it takes the name ''Noctur'nal pollution, Exoneiro'sis, Oneirog'mos, Oneirog'onos, Gonorrhœ'a dormien'tium, G. oneirog'onos, G. Vera, G. libidino'sa, Proflu'vium Sem'inis, Spermatorrhœ'a, Paronir'ia salax, Night pollution.''
# Moral or spiritual [[corruption]]; [[impurity]], [[degradation]], [[defilement]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|text=She condescended to wait on them at Pemberley, in spite of that '''pollution''' which its woods had received.}}

